  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "425c5804",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Read the table with them. Poland has the lowest nominal price (about 2.52 EUR) but the highest price relative to income. Finance and the country manager are both right about a fact and both wrong about what it implies. The data cannot settle \"too high\" on its own: we need the elasticity, the margin and an honest statement of uncertainty. That is the whole session.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why marketing mix modelling is back\n",
    "\n",
    "- Marketing is the largest discretionary budget in most consumer companies\n",
    "- User-level tracking broke: cookie deprecation, Apple's App Tracking Transparency, GDPR\n",
    "- Multi-touch attribution needs the tracking that no longer exists\n",
    "- MMM uses aggregate data (weekly sales, spend, price) that privacy rules do not touch\n",
    "- Open-source tools (PyMC-Marketing, Robyn, Meridian) made it cheap to run\n",
    "- International firms need one method that works the same in six markets\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Give the two-minute history: MMM was invented in the 1960s for packaged goods, lost ground to digital attribution in the 2010s because click data looked more precise, and returned after 2020 because that precision depended on tracking individuals. Aggregate weekly data are privacy-safe. The international angle is our angle: a board allocating a budget across countries needs comparable numbers, and MMM is the only method that produces them for TV, out-of-home and digital alike.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## What this course is, and is not\n",
    "\n",
    "- It is a course about decisions: price, promotion, budget allocation across countries\n",
    "- Regression is the one language: MMM, forecasting, experiments, attribution are all regressions with different design matrices\n",
    "- It is not a programming course: an AI assistant writes most code\n",
    "- It is a course about checking code and numbers you did not write yourself\n",
    "- Assessment: 4 quizzes (best 3), 2 lab check-ins, group project, peer evaluation\n",
    "- Reading: Yildirim and Kübler (2025); Schwarz, Chapman and Feit (2020)\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Point to the syllabus for the exact weights and the \"to align\" items. Repeat the assessment logic: quizzes test concepts without code, check-ins test that you can run and read an analysis, the project tests whether you can defend a decision.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "# The analytics workflow\n",
    "\n",
    "## Question, data, model, check, decide\n",
    "\n",
    "| Step | Today's example |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Question | Is Polish demand sensitive enough to justify a price cut? |\n",
    "| Data | Weekly units and price for six countries, 2023 to 2025 |\n",
    "| Model | log(units) on log(price), promotion, seasonality, trend |\n",
    "| Check | Sign and size plausible? CI width? Holdout fit? Collinearity? |\n",
    "| Decide | Cut, hold, or test; and say how sure you are |\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "This table is the skeleton of every lab in the course. Make the students notice that \"check\" sits between model and decide. Most mistakes in practice come from skipping it: a coefficient with the wrong sign gets reported, a model that fits 2023 but not 2025 gets used for 2027.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The decision drives the model, not the other way round\n",
    "\n",
    "- Start from the decision and the number that would change it\n",
    "- \"Too high\" means: a cut would raise profit, not only units\n",
    "- Needed: price elasticity, promotion response, margin, uncertainty\n",
    "- A model that is not connected to a decision is a hobby\n",
    "- Translate every coefficient back into euros or percentage points\n",
    "- Write the one-sentence answer before you write the code\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "A good exercise to run live: ask the class what elasticity would make the 10 % cut pay for itself. If the gross margin is about 40 %, units need to rise by roughly 33 % for profit to hold, so the elasticity would need to be around minus 3. That number frames everything they estimate later.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "# The toolkit\n",
    "\n",
    "## Positron\n",
    "\n",
    "- One window: editor, console, variables pane, plots pane, terminal\n",
    "- Open the course folder, select the `.venv` interpreter at the top right\n",
    "- Run a code cell with Ctrl+Enter (Cmd+Enter on macOS)\n",
    "- Variables pane shows every DataFrame; click to open a data viewer\n",
    "- Quarto preview renders the notebook to HTML next to the code\n",
    "- The assistant lives in the side bar and sees your open files\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Do a one-minute live demo: open lab.qmd, run the setup cell, show the DataFrame in the data viewer, render. The lab repeats this as a guided tour, so keep the lecture version short.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Quarto\n",
    "\n",
    "- A `.qmd` file is Markdown text plus executable code cells\n",
    "- Rendering runs the code and produces HTML, PDF, Word or slides\n",
    "- Prose explains the decision; code produces the evidence; both stay together\n",
    "- Labs, exercises, your project report and these slides are all Quarto\n",
    "- `#|` lines at the top of a cell set options, for example `#| echo: false`\n",
    "- Reproducible: anyone who clones your repo can re-render your report\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "The reproducibility argument matters for the group project. The grader renders the repository; if it does not render, there is no report. Mention that these slides are a Quarto file that also renders to PowerPoint, which is the format the board will expect in Session 5.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## GitHub\n",
    "\n",
    "- A repository is a folder with a complete history of every change\n",
    "- Clone once, then: pull, work, commit, push\n",
    "- Commit small and often, with a message that says what changed\n",
    "- GitHub Desktop does all of this with buttons; the lab walks you through it\n",
    "- Your group project is a repository; commit history is part of the grade\n",
    "- Conflicts happen when two people edit the same lines: ask the assistant to explain the markers\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Students fear Git. Reassure them: four verbs and one rule (pull before you start, push when you stop). Tell them the first commit in the group repository is a deliverable of Session 1.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## AI assistants: what they do well\n",
    "\n",
    "- Translate a clear request into working pandas and statsmodels code\n",
    "- Explain an error message and propose a fix\n",
    "- Explain a regression table line by line\n",
    "- Refactor repetitive code into a loop or a function\n",
    "- Draft prose from a table of results\n",
    "- Remember the conventions of this course if you tell them (log-log, Fourier terms, relative paths)\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Show one example prompt from the lab. The claim is not that the assistant is always right; it is that specifying and checking is a far better use of the student's time than typing syntax.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## AI assistants: what they do badly\n",
    "\n",
    "- Invent column names, functions and package arguments that do not exist\n",
    "- Quietly change the model when the first attempt fails (drop a variable, switch to levels)\n",
    "- Report a coefficient with the wrong sign without comment\n",
    "- Confuse statistical significance with business relevance\n",
    "- Produce a confident interpretation of a model it has not actually run\n",
    "- Forget the working directory, so paths break when you render\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Each of these appears in the labs as a \"what to verify\" item. The most dangerous one is the second: the assistant fixes an error by changing the specification, and the student does not notice that the model no longer answers the question.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Course rules on AI use\n",
    "\n",
    "1. You are responsible for every number you report; verify with the checks taught in class\n",
    "2. Document the prompts that produced non-trivial code in a short prompt log in your notebook\n",
    "3. Quizzes are closed-book and test understanding, not code\n",
    "4. Fabricated references or results are academic misconduct under WU rules\n",
    "\n",
    "- Any assistant is fine: Positron Assistant, GitHub Copilot, Claude Code\n",
    "- When in doubt, paste the output and ask the assistant to find what is wrong with it\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "These four rules are copied from the syllabus. The prompt log is a small habit with a large effect: it makes the student re-read what they asked for, and it lets the grader see the reasoning. Make clear that the rule against fabricated results includes numbers typed from memory instead of computed.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "# Regression refresher on the Alpenglow data\n",
    "\n",
    "## The data: a weekly country panel"
   ]
  },
