   "source": [
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
    "ax.scatter(de.log_price, de.log_sales, s=14, alpha=0.7)\n",
    "m0 = smf.ols(\"log_sales ~ log_price\", data=de).fit()\n",
    "xs = np.linspace(de.log_price.min(), de.log_price.max(), 50)\n",
    "ax.plot(xs, m0.params.Intercept + m0.params.log_price * xs, color=\"tab:red\", lw=2,\n",
    "        label=f\"OLS: slope = {m0.params.log_price:.2f}\")\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"log(price per unit, EUR)\"); ax.set_ylabel(\"log(units sold, thousand)\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Germany, 156 weeks\"); ax.legend(frameon=False)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bba29797",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "A scatter plot before any regression, always. The slope is about minus 2: a 1 % higher price goes with about 2 % fewer units. Ask the class whether they believe that number. Then point at the vertical spread: price explains very little of the week-to-week variation. We will find out that this slope is wrong for an interesting reason.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Ordinary least squares in one slide\n",
    "\n",
    "- Model: $\\log y_t = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 \\log p_t + \\varepsilon_t$\n",
    "- OLS picks $\\beta_0, \\beta_1$ to minimise the sum of squared residuals $\\sum_t \\hat\\varepsilon_t^2$\n",
    "- $\\hat\\beta_1$ is the slope of the best-fitting line; $\\hat\\beta_0$ the intercept\n",
    "- Each estimate comes with a standard error: how much it would move in a new sample\n",
    "- Everything else in the output (t, p, confidence interval) is derived from the estimate and its standard error\n",
    "- In Python: `smf.ols(\"log_sales ~ log_price\", data=de).fit()`\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Keep the maths to one line. The formula syntax is the thing to learn: left of the tilde is the outcome, right of the tilde are the explanatory variables, and statsmodels adds the intercept. Everything they estimate this term uses this syntax.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Reading a statsmodels summary {.smaller}"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
