  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "26dde476",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Walk through the table slowly. Top left: dependent variable, 156 observations. Top right: R-squared 0.15. Middle: one row per coefficient with coef, standard error, t, p, and the 95 % interval. Bottom: Durbin-Watson 0.24 indicates strongly autocorrelated residuals, which tells us the model is missing something that moves slowly, such as seasonality or a trend. The condition number warning is harmless here.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The coefficient table, column by column\n",
    "\n",
    "| Column | Meaning | Germany, simple model |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| coef | Estimated effect of a one-unit change in x on y | -2.02 |\n",
    "| std err | Uncertainty of the estimate | 0.39 |\n",
    "| t | coef divided by std err | -5.2 |\n",
    "| P>\\|t\\| | Probability of a t this large if the true coef were 0 | 0.000 |\n",
    "| [0.025, 0.975] | 95 % confidence interval: coef ± about 2 std err | -2.79 to -1.24 |\n",
    "\n",
    "- Rule of thumb: |t| > 2 means the interval excludes zero at the 5 % level\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Students know p-values from their bachelor's degree, but usually not the confidence interval. Push the interval: it is the honest statement of what we know. Here the interval says the elasticity is somewhere between minus 2.8 and minus 1.2, which already spans a large range of business implications.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## R-squared: what it does and does not tell you\n",
    "\n",
    "- R² = share of the variance of y explained by the model, 0 to 1\n",
    "- Simple German model: 0.15; the full model later today: 0.95\n",
    "- A high R² does not mean the coefficients are right\n",
    "- A low R² does not mean the price coefficient is useless\n",
    "- Adjusted R² penalises extra variables; use it to compare models of different size\n",
    "- The real test of a model is how it predicts data it has not seen\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Seasonality alone pushes R-squared from 0.15 to over 0.9 in this data without changing what we learn about price. Conversely, the first model with promotion has a decent R-squared and a price coefficient with the wrong sign. Get the class used to reading R-squared as \"how much of the wiggle is accounted for\", not as a quality seal.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Log-log means elasticity\n",
    "\n",
    "- $\\log y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 \\log p$ implies $\\beta_1 = \\frac{\\partial \\log y}{\\partial \\log p} = \\frac{\\% \\Delta y}{\\% \\Delta p}$\n",
    "- $\\beta_1 = -1.4$: a 1 % price increase lowers units by about 1.4 %\n",
    "- $|\\beta_1| > 1$: elastic, a price cut raises revenue; $|\\beta_1| < 1$: inelastic\n",
    "- Elasticities are unit-free, so they can be compared across countries and currencies\n",
    "- A dummy variable in a log model: effect is $e^{\\beta} - 1$, roughly $\\beta$ for small $\\beta$\n",
    "- A share such as promo_share: $\\beta \\times 0.10$ is the effect of 10 percentage points more promotion\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "This is the most important slide of the refresher. Make sure every student can convert a log-log coefficient to a percentage statement and a dummy coefficient to a percentage uplift. Note that revenue-maximising is not profit-maximising: with a 40 % margin, the cut that raises revenue may still cut profit.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Add promotion: the sign flips"
   ]
  },
