   "source": [
    "steps = {\n",
    "    \"price only\": \"log_sales ~ log_price\",\n",
    "    \"+ promo\": \"log_sales ~ log_price + promo_share\",\n",
    "    \"+ holidays\": \"log_sales ~ log_price + promo_share + xmas + easter + valentine\",\n",
    "    \"+ Fourier\": \"log_sales ~ log_price + promo_share + xmas + easter + valentine + sin_1 + cos_1 + sin_2 + cos_2\",\n",
    "    \"+ trend\": FULL,\n",
    "}\n",
    "rows = []\n",
    "for name, f in steps.items():\n",
    "    m = smf.ols(f, data=de).fit()\n",
    "    rows.append({\"Model\": name, \"Price coef\": round(m.params.log_price, 2),\n",
    "                 \"Std err\": round(m.bse.log_price, 2),\n",
    "                 \"Promo coef\": round(m.params.get(\"promo_share\", np.nan), 2),\n",
    "                 \"R²\": round(m.rsquared, 3)})\n",
    "pd.DataFrame(rows).set_index(\"Model\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ff598f0f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- Adding promotion share turns the German price coefficient from -2.0 to +1.2\n",
    "- Seasonality lifts R² to 0.92 but the sign stays wrong\n",
    "- Only the time trend restores a negative elasticity\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Build suspense: the assistant would happily report the +1.2 if asked. Why does it happen? Promotions cut the price and raise sales at the same time, so price and promotion share are correlated at about minus 0.75. Once promotion is in the model, the remaining price variation is mostly inflation, and inflation goes along with the growth of the brand. Without a trend, the model attributes growth to higher prices. The fix is on the next slides.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why: price moves with promotion and with time"
   ]
  },
