  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bb52534a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Two sources of price variation: promotional dips (left to right) and inflation (dark to light). The promotional dips are also weeks with more sales because of the promotion itself, not only because of the lower price. Inflation runs parallel to brand growth. A regression can only separate these if both confounders are in the model. This is the omitted-variable logic that returns in Session 2 as \"budget follows seasonality\".\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Seasonality: holiday dummies and Fourier terms\n",
    "\n",
    "- Chocolate peaks before Christmas, Easter and Valentine's Day: three 0/1 dummies\n",
    "- The smooth annual cycle: $\\sin(2\\pi k t/52.18)$ and $\\cos(2\\pi k t/52.18)$ for $k = 1, 2$\n",
    "- Two Fourier pairs capture most of a yearly curve with four coefficients instead of 51 week dummies\n",
    "- `mma.add_fourier(df, order=2)` adds the columns `sin_1, cos_1, sin_2, cos_2`\n",
    "- Trend: a linear term in `t` in a log model is a constant growth rate\n",
    "- German trend coefficient 0.0020 per week, about 11 % per year\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "The Fourier idea is new to most students. Draw a sine wave on the board: one pair gives a single annual hump, the second pair sharpens the shape. Explain why 51 weekly dummies would overfit on three years of data: each dummy is estimated from three observations. The lab compares both approaches on holdout data.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The full model for one country (Germany) {.smaller}"
   ]
  },
