  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c3a836f6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Point estimates run from about minus 0.9 in Austria and the Netherlands to minus 1.4 in Germany and Italy; Poland sits at minus 1.2. Every interval overlaps every other interval. The honest reading: all markets are around unit elastic, and three years of weekly data cannot rank them reliably on price. The lab produces this table with the exact numbers.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Dummies and interactions: one model, six slopes\n",
    "\n",
    "- Country dummies shift the intercept: $\\log y = \\alpha_c + \\ldots$\n",
    "- An interaction lets the slope differ: $\\beta_{\\text{price}} + \\gamma_c \\cdot \\text{country}_c$\n",
    "- Formula: `log_sales ~ C(country) * log_price + ...`\n",
    "- Reference country is the first alphabetically (AT); each $\\gamma_c$ is the difference from Austria\n",
    "- Pooled data give 936 observations; common seasonality is estimated more precisely\n",
    "- A t-test on $\\gamma_{DE} - \\gamma_{PL}$ answers \"do Germany and Poland differ?\"\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Students often estimate six separate regressions and then compare coefficients by eye. The interaction model does the comparison with a standard error. Make sure they can read the output: log_price is the Austrian elasticity, C(country)[T.PL]:log_price is Poland minus Austria.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Pooled model with country × log price"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "de4debdd",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 768x384 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "pooled = smf.ols(\"log_sales ~ C(country) * log_price + C(country):promo_share + C(country):t \"\n",
    "                 \"+ xmas + easter + valentine + sin_1 + cos_1 + sin_2 + cos_2\", data=df).fit()\n",
    "base = pooled.params[\"log_price\"]\n",
    "rows = [{\"country\": \"AT\", \"elasticity\": base}]\n",
    "for c in COUNTRIES[1:]:\n",
    "    rows.append({\"country\": c, \"elasticity\": base + pooled.params[f\"C(country)[T.{c}]:log_price\"]})\n",
    "pe = pd.DataFrame(rows).set_index(\"country\")\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
    "x = np.arange(len(COUNTRIES))\n",
    "ax.bar(x - 0.2, el.elasticity, width=0.4, label=\"Six separate regressions\", color=\"tab:blue\")\n",
    "ax.bar(x + 0.2, pe.elasticity, width=0.4, label=\"Pooled with interactions\", color=\"tab:orange\")\n",
    "ax.set_xticks(x); ax.set_xticklabels(COUNTRIES)\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"Price elasticity\"); ax.legend(frameon=False)\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Same slopes, two ways of estimating them\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "666c1cb0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "When the pooled model lets promotion and trend differ by country too, it reproduces the six separate elasticities almost exactly. The joint F-test for \"all interaction terms are zero\" does not reject (p about 0.73). If instead you force one promotion effect and one trend on all countries, the Polish elasticity comes out positive again, because Poland's 28 % growth is squeezed into a common trend. Pooling is powerful and dangerous in equal measure; Session 3 makes this the central theme.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Multicollinearity and the VIF"
   ]
  },
