   "source": [
    "cols = [\"log_price\", \"promo_share\", \"xmas\", \"easter\", \"valentine\", \"sin_1\", \"cos_1\", \"sin_2\", \"cos_2\", \"t\"]\n",
    "X = sm.add_constant(de[cols])\n",
    "vif = pd.Series([variance_inflation_factor(X.values, i) for i in range(X.shape[1])], index=X.columns)\n",
    "vif.drop(\"const\").round(2).to_frame(\"VIF\").T"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "aaa499b8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- VIF = 1 / (1 - R² of regressing that variable on all the others)\n",
    "- Rule of thumb: above 5 is worth a look, above 10 is a problem\n",
    "- Price (10.5) and promotion (7.1) are collinear because promotions cut the price\n",
    "- Consequence: wider standard errors, not biased coefficients\n",
    "- Cure: more variation (a price test), not dropping a variable that belongs in the model\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Students tend to drop a variable to fix a VIF. Push back: dropping promotion gave us the wrong sign. Collinearity is the data telling us that price and promotion rarely move independently, so the model cannot tell them apart precisely. The confidence interval already reports that. The real cure is a price experiment, which is Session 4 material.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## In-sample fit versus holdout fit (Poland)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "492a9426",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 768x384 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "train = pl[pl.year < 2025]; test = pl[pl.year == 2025]\n",
    "m_tr = smf.ols(FULL, data=train).fit()\n",
    "pred = np.exp(m_tr.predict(test))\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
    "ax.plot(pl.week, pl.sales_units_k, color=\"tab:gray\", lw=1.2, label=\"Actual\")\n",
    "ax.plot(train.week, np.exp(m_tr.fittedvalues), color=\"tab:blue\", lw=1.2, label=\"Fitted 2023 to 2024\")\n",
    "ax.plot(test.week, pred, color=\"tab:red\", lw=1.5, label=\"Predicted 2025 (holdout)\")\n",
    "ax.axvline(pd.Timestamp(\"2025-01-01\"), color=\"black\", ls=\":\", lw=1)\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"Units (thousand)\"); ax.set_xlabel(\"\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(f\"Poland: MAPE in-sample {mape(np.exp(m_tr.fittedvalues), train.sales_units_k):.1f} %, \"\n",
    "             f\"holdout {mape(pred, test.sales_units_k):.1f} %\")\n",
    "ax.legend(frameon=False); plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1825b89a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Fit the model on 2023 and 2024, predict 2025. The mean absolute percentage error rises only from about 4 % to 5 %: the model generalises. Contrast with the lab's overfitting demo: twelve Fourier pairs plus 52 week dummies push the in-sample error below 2 % and the holdout error above 15 %. R-squared cannot see that; a holdout can. Every model in this course gets a holdout check.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Correlation is not causation\n",
    "\n",
    "- The price coefficient is causal only if nothing left out moves both price and sales\n",
    "- We saw one omission (promotion) flip the sign and another (trend) flip it back\n",
    "- Still missing today: media spend, competitor activity, distribution\n",
    "- Prices are set by managers who look at demand: reverse causality\n",
    "- Experiments break this circle: Session 4 covers geo-lift tests\n",
    "- Until then: report elasticities as \"associations under the model's assumptions\"\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Preview Session 4 without teaching it. The geo-lift test in Germany is an experiment where the firm chose which regions get more spend at random, so the comparison is causal by design. For price, the equivalent is a regional price test. Mention that the media omitted today is exactly why Session 2 exists; the instructor solutions show how its omission biases today's elasticities towards zero.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Elasticities and country characteristics"
   ]
  },
