   "source": [
    "mm = el.join(meta.set_index(\"country\"))\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(8, 4), sharey=True)\n",
    "for ax, var, lab in zip(axes, [\"gdp_per_capita_eur_k\", \"hofstede_uai\"],\n",
    "                        [\"GDP per capita (thousand EUR)\", \"Hofstede uncertainty avoidance\"]):\n",
    "    ax.errorbar(mm[var], mm.elasticity, yerr=[mm.elasticity - mm.lo, mm.hi - mm.elasticity],\n",
    "                fmt=\"o\", capsize=4, color=\"tab:blue\", ecolor=\"tab:gray\")\n",
    "    for c, r in mm.iterrows():\n",
    "        ax.annotate(c, (r[var], r.elasticity), textcoords=\"offset points\", xytext=(6, 4), fontsize=9)\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel(lab)\n",
    "axes[0].set_ylabel(\"Price elasticity (95 % CI)\")\n",
    "fig.suptitle(\"Does price sensitivity follow income or culture?\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "08a8187b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Six points and wide intervals: this is a conversation starter, not a test. Economic theory predicts more elastic demand where the product takes a larger share of income, which would put Poland at the bottom. Culture theory predicts that high uncertainty avoidance favours known brands and lowers price sensitivity, which would put Poland and France higher. In the data, Poland is in the middle, Italy and Germany most elastic. The next slide turns this into a discussion.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Discussion: what moderates price sensitivity across countries?\n",
    "\n",
    "- Income: the same price is a larger share of a Polish budget; theory says more elastic\n",
    "- Market maturity: Alpenglow is 4 years old in Poland, 40 in Austria; young brands have less loyalty\n",
    "- Culture: high uncertainty avoidance (PL 93, FR 86) favours trusted brands; collectivism favours shared, visible brands\n",
    "- Retail structure: concentrated retail (DE 0.85) means fewer, larger promotions\n",
    "- Category: premium chocolate is a gift as much as a snack, and gifts are less price-sensitive\n",
    "- With six countries, which of these could you ever separate statistically?\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Run this as a five-minute discussion. The honest answer to the last bullet is \"none of them\": six observations cannot separate five moderators. That is why the group project will use within-country variation and hierarchical models rather than cross-country regressions of elasticities on Hofstede scores. Still, the moderators matter for the story the board hears, and for judging whether an estimate is plausible.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Back to the decision: Poland\n",
    "\n",
    "- Estimated price elasticity -1.2, 95 % CI -1.8 to -0.6: around unit elastic\n",
    "- Promotion response is the strongest of the six markets: +6 % units per 10 points of promo share\n",
    "- Poland has the lowest shelf price but the highest price relative to income\n",
    "- A 10 % permanent cut would raise units by about 13 % and revenue by about 2 %, and cut profit by about 15 % at a 40 % margin\n",
    "- The model omits media and distribution, which may bias the elasticity towards zero\n",
    "- Recommendation: do not cut the list price yet; run a regional price test and keep promotion as the lever\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "Walk through the arithmetic: 0.9 to the power of minus 1.2 gives plus 13 % units; revenue is 0.9 times 1.13, about 1.02; profit per unit falls from 0.40 to 0.30 of the old price, so profit falls by roughly 15 % even with the extra volume. Break-even needs an elasticity of about minus 2.7, which the interval does not support. The team exercise asks each group to write this answer in five sentences.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "# Lab bridge\n",
    "\n",
    "## In the lab today\n",
    "\n",
    "- Positron tour and your first commit with GitHub Desktop\n",
    "- Warm-up: regional sales shares and a first regression on video game data\n",
    "- Alpenglow: load, describe, plot sales and price by country\n",
    "- Build the log-log model step by step and watch the price sign flip and flip back\n",
    "- Six elasticities with confidence intervals, pooled model, VIF, 2025 holdout\n",
    "- Merge with country metadata and write the Poland answer as a team\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
    "The lab is designed to be run cell by cell with the assistant open. Every section has a prompt pattern and a \"what to verify\" list. The team exercise at the end produces the five-sentence answer to the decision of the day.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Recap\n",
    "\n",
    "- Decision first: the question defines the model and the number that matters\n",
    "- OLS: coef, standard error, t, p and confidence interval; read the interval\n",
    "- Log-log coefficients are elasticities; dummies are percentage uplifts\n",
    "- Omitted variables bias coefficients; promotion and trend both mattered\n",
    "- Interactions give country-specific slopes with a standard error for the difference\n",
    "- VIF measures collinearity; holdout fit measures usefulness; neither is R²\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.notes}\n",
